1
00:00:01,110 --> 00:00:05,060
This timeline began as one
request to an AI assistant:

2
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:08,060
create a timeline of the
Star Wars extended history,

3
00:00:08,110 --> 00:00:15,460
with comics, Legends, games, TV and books.
The assistant works through the HTTP

4
00:00:15,510 --> 00:00:18,623
API, and for that, it needs a key.

5
00:00:20,293 --> 00:00:25,183
In Account, API keys, make a key
that can read and write timelines,

6
00:00:25,233 --> 00:00:30,323
and give it to the assistant. It reaches
the timeline endpoints and nothing

7
00:00:30,373 --> 00:00:34,573
else, a hundred and twenty
requests a minute by default,

8
00:00:34,623 --> 00:00:39,643
and it expires. A key is a password:
give it only to tools you trust,

9
00:00:39,693 --> 00:00:43,781
allow only what the job needs,
and revoke it when you're done.

10
00:00:45,341 --> 00:00:50,111
First, it downloaded an existing
timeline to learn the format.

11
00:00:50,161 --> 00:00:54,691
Then it wrote its own: years counted
before and after the Battle of Yavin,

12
00:00:54,741 --> 00:01:01,091
the eras as durations, and every moment
tagged by continuity and by medium.

13
00:01:01,141 --> 00:01:05,433
One POST created it, private,
as every new timeline starts.

14
00:01:07,173 --> 00:01:11,483
Later, it added the Thrawn books,
the Tales series and Visions,

15
00:01:11,533 --> 00:01:17,133
each a PUT against the current revision,
so it couldn't overwrite a newer save.

16
00:01:17,183 --> 00:01:20,117
Then one PATCH made it
public, at a short address.

17
00:01:21,877 --> 00:01:26,707
Here it is: ninety-eight moments,
thirteen eras and twenty relationships,

18
00:01:26,757 --> 00:01:30,107
from Tython, thirty-six
thousand years before Yavin,

19
00:01:30,157 --> 00:01:34,207
to the films. It's an
unofficial fan chronology,

20
00:01:34,257 --> 00:01:37,124
and plenty of its dates are approximate.

21
00:01:38,844 --> 00:01:43,794
Where it wasn't sure, the assistant
marked the date approximate and said why.

22
00:01:43,844 --> 00:01:48,724
Every save is kept in History,
so a bad edit can be put right,

23
00:01:48,774 --> 00:01:52,504
and anyone signed in can propose
a fix as a pull request.

24
00:01:52,554 --> 00:01:56,794
A person asked, gave the
key and checked the result.

25
00:01:56,844 --> 00:02:01,027
Review what an AI makes
like anyone's contribution.

26
00:02:02,637 --> 00:02:07,007
The same kind of key drives the log
importer, the syslog and journal receivers,

27
00:02:07,057 --> 00:02:11,997
and any script: curl, Python,
a cron job, a CI pipeline,

28
00:02:12,047 --> 00:02:18,877
a notebook. The API reference and its
OpenAPI description cover every endpoint,

29
00:02:18,927 --> 00:02:21,667
for code generators and agents.

30
00:02:23,217 --> 00:02:28,907
OpenChronology itself is built this
way. Most of its code is written by AI

31
00:02:28,957 --> 00:02:32,687
coding agents: Anthropic's
Claude, through Claude Code,

32
00:02:32,737 --> 00:02:37,647
and OpenAI's models. One maintainer
decides what gets built,

33
00:02:37,697 --> 00:02:42,847
reviews and merges every pull
request, and runs the servers.

34
00:02:42,897 --> 00:02:48,147
Agents make mistakes, so every
pull request runs the tests in CI.

35
00:02:48,197 --> 00:02:52,427
When the Agree button on the terms broke,
the fix came with a browser test that

36
00:02:52,477 --> 00:02:53,712
presses it.

37
00:02:55,432 --> 00:02:59,222
Your timelines, built by you,
your scripts, or your assistant,

38
00:02:59,272 --> 00:03:01,651
on a server you can run yourself.
